s demonstrate that the control was implemented and effective?\n5. Describe a realistic failure scenario, immediate safe response, escalation path and learning review.\n\nModel response approach: explain the sequence (plan - assess - authorize - brief - verify - monitor - close), name the accountable role, cite the approved site procedure/standard rather than guessing a numeric limit, and give a practical example with evidence and corrective-action follow-up.\n\nK. Non-conformance and continual improvement\nClassify findings by actual and potential consequence using the site\'s approved system. Apply immediate risk reduction, assign an accountable owner and due date, identify underlying system causes, verify effectiveness at the workface, and feed lessons into risk assessments, training, procurement, maintenance and management review. Close an action only when objective evidence confirms the risk is controlled.''' ),
  HseTopic(number: 33, title: 'Radiation Safety & Non-Destructive Testing', content: '''TOPIC 33 – Radiation Safety & Non-Destructive Testing

Purpose
Protect workers and the public from ionizing and non-ionizing radiation during NDT and other radiation-generating work.

Principal hazards and exposure pathways
Uncontrolled X-ray/gamma exposure, source loss, unauthorized entry, inadequate survey, poor shielding and dose-monitoring failure.

Technical requirements and field application
1. Establish task scope, responsible persons, competence requirements and acceptance criteria before work.
2. Complete task-specific risk assessment with workers and affected interfaces; consider routine, non-routine, abnormal and emergency conditions.
3. Apply the hierarchy of controls: eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, then PPE. Verify controls at the workface.
4. Use approved procedures, permits, isolation and competent authorization where applicable. A permit or checklist alone does not make work safe.
5. Confirm equipment suitability, inspection status, environmental conditions, communication and supervision before release.
6. Monitor changing conditions. Stop, make safe and reassess after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, alarms or interface changes.
7. Provide emergency arrangements appropriate to the credible scenario; do not expose untrained personnel to rescue hazards.
8. Record deficiencies with immediate safeguards, accountable owner, due date and effectiveness verification.
9. Resume only after competent review and authorization; communicate revised controls to all affected workers.

Topic-specific controls
Identify radiation type and source; use licensed/authorized organizations and competent radiation protection personnel as required. Apply time, distance and shielding; establish controlled area, barriers, warning lights/signs, access control and communications. Survey before, during and after exposure using suitable calibrated instruments. Use personal dosimetry and maintain dose records. Verify source security and inventory. If source is suspected stuck, lost or damaged, stop, withdraw, cordon area and notify radiation protection lead and authorities through approved emergency procedure; never approach or attempt recovery without authorization.

Documents, records and field verification
Radiography plan/permit, source inventory and movement logs, survey records, dosimetry, instrument calibration, competency and emergency response records.
- Verify documents against actual site conditions; retain controlled revision, approval, inspection and closeout evidence.
- Record who checked the control, when, location, result, defects and action closure.

UAE / project application
Apply current UAE federal requirements, relevant emirate authority rules, client/operator standards and approved project procedures. Confirm the latest official edition and applicability. Numeric limits, exposure criteria, design values and inspection intervals must come from current authoritative requirements, manufacturer instructions and competent engineering assessment; this educational reference does not establish universal legal limits.

Practical site scenario
A boundary alarm fails during radiography. Stop exposure if safe under procedure, evacuate and secure area, survey with competent RPO and do not resume until boundary controls are restored.

Interview and competency questions
1. What are the main hazards and who may be exposed?
2. Which controls are critical, and how will you verify them physically?
3. What documents, permits, competencies or equipment checks are required before work?
4. What conditions require stop-work, escalation or emergency activation?
5. How will you confirm corrective action effectiveness before restart?

Field assessment
Ask the learner to prepare a task risk assessment, identify critical barriers, conduct a pre-start field verification, explain emergency actions and demonstrate a safe handover/closeout.\n\nFIELD-READY GOLD STANDARD EXPANSION\nRadiography/NDT: establish controlled area from approved radiation protection assessment; appoint authorized radiation protection personnel, verify source security, survey instruments and dosimetry, warning signs/barriers and access control. Apply time-distance-shielding, emergency source recovery plan and statutory notification/reporting requirements. Never handle a suspected stuck source without specialist response.''\n\nDETAILED GOLD STANDARD FIELD IMPLEMENTATION - TOPIC 33\n\nA. Planning and applicability\nDefine the work boundary, lifecycle stage, people exposed, simultaneous activities, operating status and interfaces before mobilization. Identify applicable UAE federal requirements, emirate authority rules, client/operator standards, contract specifications and approved engineering documents. Maintain a compliance register with source, applicability rationale, owner, evidence and review date. Do not treat guidance or a client rule as legislation; verify current editions and site-specific requirements.\n\nB. Competence and accountability\nThe responsible manager provides resources and confirms the approved method. The supervisor briefs the work party, checks competency and verifies controls at the workface. The HSE Officer advises, samples compliance, records deficiencies and escalates imminent danger; operational control remains with the designated line authority. Workers stop and report unsafe conditions. Appoint competent specialists where engineering, inspection, medical, environmental or emergency expertise is required.\n\nC. Task risk assessment\nBreak the job into sequential steps. For each step identify routine, non-routine and foreseeable abnormal conditions; exposed persons; initiating events; credible consequences; existing barriers; residual risk; and named action owners. Apply elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative controls and PPE in that order. Record assumptions, interfaces and trigger conditions requiring reassessment. Revalidate after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, site condition or simultaneous-operations changes.\n\nD. Authorization and work pack\nBefore work, confirm approved risk assessment/JSA, method statement, permits where required, drawings, isolation certificates, competency records, equipment certificates, inspection status, emergency arrangements and toolbox-talk attendance. Check document revision and physical work location. Resolve conflicting permits and SIMOPS through the site coordination process. Authorization is not proof that field conditions remain safe; pause and revalidate when conditions change.\n\nE. Execution and critical-control verification\nConduct a point-of-work walkdown with the crew. Verify each critical control physically, not only by paperwork; identify who checks it, when, and what evidence is retained. Confirm access, exclusion zones, communications, housekeeping, environmental protection and safe access/egress. Use hold points before irreversible or high-consequence steps. If a critical barrier is absent, impaired or uncertain, stop the affected task, make the area safe, notify the responsible supervisor and restart only after documented correction and re-authorization.\n\nF. Inspection, monitoring and records\nSet inspection frequency from law, manufacturer instructions, engineering basis, risk, use intensity and client procedure; never invent a universal interval. Record asset/task identity, location, date, competent inspector, acceptance criteria/source, findings, defects, immediate controls, corrective-action owner and closeout evidence. Quarantine defective equipment where relevant. Retain permits, assessments, briefings, inspection reports, calibration/certification evidence, photographs and closeout records under the approved retention schedule.\n\nG. Emergency and abnormal conditions\nIdentify credible emergency scenarios for this topic, alarm method, emergency contact, muster/evacuation route, access for responders, first-aid provision and rescue capability. Define stop-work triggers and safe shutdown/isolation actions. Do not initiate improvised rescue or recovery that exposes additional people; use trained responders, approved equipment and the site emergency plan. After an event, preserve evidence where safe, report promptly, support investigation and track corrective actions to verified closure.\n\nH. Supervisor / HSE Officer field checklist\n[ ] Scope and latest approved documents match the actual task.\n[ ] Competency, fitness and role assignments are confirmed.\n[ ] Risk controls are understood by the crew in a language they understand.\n[ ] Critical barriers are physically verified and recorded.\n[ ] Permit, isolation and interface conditions are valid where applicable.\n[ ] Equipment and work area meet documented acceptance criteria.\n[ ] Emergency communication, access and response resources are ready.\n[ ] Changes, defects and stop-work events are escalated and closed before restart.\n\nI. Practical UAE site application\nAt a UAE construction, industrial or energy site, coordinate the task with the permit office, area authority, contractor supervisor and affected operations. Consider heat, dust, visibility, language diversity, welfare, access control and nearby simultaneous work where relevant. Apply the approved site heat-stress, emergency, environmental and traffic arrangements; check current authority and client requirements rather than assuming one rule applies across all emirates or sectors.\n\nJ. Interview and assurance questions\n1. How do you establish which legal, authority, client and project requirements apply? Explain the evidence you would check.\n2. What are the task\'s critical controls, and how would you verify them in the field?\n3. What changes would make you stop, reassess and re-authorize the work?\n4. What records demonstrate that the control was implemented and effective?\n5. Describe a realistic failure scenario, immediate safe response, escalation path and learning review.\n\nModel response approach: explain the sequence (plan - assess - authorize - brief - verify - monitor - close), name the accountable role, cite the approved site procedure/standard rather than guessing a numeric limit, and give a practical example with evidence and corrective-action follow-up.\n\nK. Non-conformance and continual improvement\nClassify findings by actual and potential consequence using the site\'s approved system. Apply immediate risk reduction, assign an accountable owner and due date, identify underlying system causes, verify effectiveness at the workface, and feed lessons into risk assessments, training, procurement, maintenance and management review. Close an action only when objective evidence confirms the risk is controlled.''' ),
  HseTopic(number: 34, title: 'Dropped Objects Prevention & Overhead Work', content: '''TOPIC 34 – Dropped Objects Prevention & Overhead Work

Purpose
Prevent injury and asset damage from tools, materials or components falling from height or moving overhead.

Principal hazards and exposure pathways
Unsecured tools, loose materials, lifting loads, wind, dropped fasteners, failed toe boards and work above occupied areas.

Technical requirements and field application
1. Establish task scope, responsible persons, competence requirements and acceptance criteria before work.
2. Complete task-specific risk assessment with workers and affected interfaces; consider routine, non-routine, abnormal and emergency conditions.
3. Apply the hierarchy of controls: eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, then PPE. Verify controls at the workface.
4. Use approved procedures, permits, isolation and competent authorization where applicable. A permit or checklist alone does not make work safe.
5. Confirm equipment suitability, inspection status, environmental conditions, communication and supervision before release.
6. Monitor changing conditions. Stop, make safe and reassess after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, alarms or interface changes.
7. Provide emergency arrangements appropriate to the credible scenario; do not expose untrained personnel to rescue hazards.
8. Record deficiencies with immediate safeguards, accountable owner, due date and effectiveness verification.
9. Resume only after competent review and authorization; communicate revised controls to all affected workers.

Topic-specific controls
Eliminate overhead work where possible; schedule exclusion zones and separate people from drop zones. Use tool lanyards, secured materials, toe boards, debris netting and closed containers appropriate to the task and engineered system. Inspect platforms and securing arrangements before shift and after change. Plan lifting, verify load security and prohibit passing loads over people. Consider wind and vibration. Establish clear communication, access control and stop-work triggers. Investigate near misses for systemic causes rather than relying only on worker caution.

Documents, records and field verification
Dropped-object risk assessment, lifting plan, tool-tether inspection, scaffold/platform inspection, exclusion-zone checks and incident/near-miss reports.
- Verify documents against actual site conditions; retain controlled revision, approval, inspection and closeout evidence.
- Record who checked the control, when, location, result, defects and action closure.

UAE / project application
Apply current UAE federal requirements, relevant emirate authority rules, client/operator standards and approved project procedures. Confirm the latest official edition and applicability. Numeric limits, exposure criteria, design values and inspection intervals must come from current authoritative requirements, manufacturer instructions and competent engineering assessment; this educational reference does not establish universal legal limits.

Practical site scenario
A spanner is found below an elevated workfront. Stop work above and below, establish exclusion zone, inspect tool retention and housekeeping, and verify all items secured before restart.

Interview and competency questions
1. What are the main hazards and who may be exposed?
2. Which controls are critical, and how will you verify them physically?
3. What documents, permits, competencies or equipment checks are required before work?
4. What conditions require stop-work, escalation or emergency activation?
5. How will you confirm corrective action effectiveness before restart?

Field assessment
Ask the learner to prepare a task risk assessment, identify critical barriers, conduct a pre-start field verification, explain emergency actions and demonstrate a safe handover/closeout.\n\nFIELD-READY GOLD STANDARD EXPANSION\nOverhead work: plan dropped-object prevention from tool/material selection through transport, installation and removal. Secure components against wind/vibration, use rated attachment points and tool tethering, protect lower levels and coordinate permit boundaries. Inspect after work and maintain positive control during handover.''\n\nDETAILED GOLD STANDARD FIELD IMPLEMENTATION - TOPIC 34\n\nA. Planning and applicability\nDefine the work boundary, lifecycle stage, people exposed, simultaneous activities, operating status and interfaces before mobilization. Identify applicable UAE federal requirements, emirate authority rules, client/operator standards, contract specifications and approved engineering documents. Maintain a compliance register with source, applicability rationale, owner, evidence and review date. Do not treat guidance or a client rule as legislation; verify current editions and site-specific requirements.\n\nB. Competence and accountability\nThe responsible manager provides resources and confirms the approved method. The supervisor briefs the work party, checks competency and verifies controls at the workface. The HSE Officer advises, samples compliance, records deficiencies and escalates imminent danger; operational control remains with the designated line authority. Workers stop and report unsafe conditions. Appoint competent specialists where engineering, inspection, medical, environmental or emergency expertise is required.\n\nC. Task risk assessment\nBreak the job into sequential steps. For each step identify routine, non-routine and foreseeable abnormal conditions; exposed persons; initiating events; credible consequences; existing barriers; residual risk; and named action owners. Apply elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative controls and PPE in that order. Record assumptions, interfaces and trigger conditions requiring reassessment. Revalidate after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, site condition or simultaneous-operations changes.\n\nD. Authorization and work pack\nBefore work, confirm approved risk assessment/JSA, method statement, permits where required, drawings, isolation certificates, competency records, equipment certificates, inspection status, emergency arrangements and toolbox-talk attendance. Check document revision and physical work location. Resolve conflicting permits and SIMOPS through the site coordination process. Authorization is not proof that field conditions remain safe; pause and revalidate when conditions change.\n\nE. Execution and critical-control verification\nConduct a point-of-work walkdown with the crew. Verify each critical control physically, not only by paperwork; identify who checks it, when, and what evidence is retained. Confirm access, exclusion zones, communications, housekeeping, environmental protection and safe access/egress. Use hold points before irreversible or high-consequence steps. If a critical barrier is absent, impaired or uncertain, stop the affected task, make the area safe, notify the responsible supervisor and restart only after documented correction and re-authorization.\n\nF. Inspection, monitoring and records\nSet inspection frequency from law, manufacturer instructions, engineering basis, risk, use intensity and client procedure; never invent a universal interval. Record asset/task identity, location, date, competent inspector, acceptance criteria/source, findings, defects, immediate controls, corrective-action owner and closeout evidence. Quarantine defective equipment where relevant. Retain permits, assessments, briefings, inspection reports, calibration/certification evidence, photographs and closeout records under the approved retention schedule.\n\nG. Emergency and abnormal conditions\nIdentify credible emergency scenarios for this topic, alarm method, emergency contact, muster/evacuation route, access for responders, first-aid provision and rescue capability. Define stop-work triggers and safe shutdown/isolation actions. Do not initiate improvised rescue or recovery that exposes additional people; use trained responders, approved equipment and the site emergency plan. After an event, preserve evidence where safe, report promptly, support investigation and track corrective actions to verified closure.\n\nH. Supervisor / HSE Officer field checklist\n[ ] Scope and latest approved documents match the actual task.\n[ ] Competency, fitness and role assignments are confirmed.\n[ ] Risk controls are understood by the crew in a language they understand.\n[ ] Critical barriers are physically verified and recorded.\n[ ] Permit, isolation and interface conditions are valid where applicable.\n[ ] Equipment and work area meet documented acceptance criteria.\n[ ] Emergency communication, access and response resources are ready.\n[ ] Changes, defects and stop-work events are escalated and closed before restart.\n\nI. Practical UAE site application\nAt a UAE construction, industrial or energy site, coordinate the task with the permit office, area authority, contractor supervisor and affected operations. Consider heat, dust, visibility, language diversity, welfare, access control and nearby simultaneous work where relevant. Apply the approved site heat-stress, emergency, environmental and traffic arrangements; check current authority and client requirements rather than assuming one rule applies across all emirates or sectors.\n\nJ. Interview and assurance questions\n1. How do you establish which legal, authority, client and project requirements apply? Explain the evidence you would check.\n2. What are the task\'s critical controls, and how would you verify them in the field?\n3. What changes would make you stop, reassess and re-authorize the work?\n4. What records demonstrate that