  HseTopic(number: 35, title: 'Marine Safety, Offshore Operations & Vessel Interface', content: '''TOPIC 35 – Marine Safety, Offshore Operations & Vessel Interface

Purpose
Manage marine and offshore risks across vessels, jetties, platforms, transfer points and contractor interfaces.

Principal hazards and exposure pathways
Man overboard, vessel collision, transfer failure, weather/sea state, mooring line snap-back, slips, fatigue and pollution.

Technical requirements and field application
1. Establish task scope, responsible persons, competence requirements and acceptance criteria before work.
2. Complete task-specific risk assessment with workers and affected interfaces; consider routine, non-routine, abnormal and emergency conditions.
3. Apply the hierarchy of controls: eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, then PPE. Verify controls at the workface.
4. Use approved procedures, permits, isolation and competent authorization where applicable. A permit or checklist alone does not make work safe.
5. Confirm equipment suitability, inspection status, environmental conditions, communication and supervision before release.
6. Monitor changing conditions. Stop, make safe and reassess after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, alarms or interface changes.
7. Provide emergency arrangements appropriate to the credible scenario; do not expose untrained personnel to rescue hazards.
8. Record deficiencies with immediate safeguards, accountable owner, due date and effectiveness verification.
9. Resume only after competent review and authorization; communicate revised controls to all affected workers.

Topic-specific controls
Use approved marine procedures and vessel/operator interface plan. Verify vessel suitability, crew competency, communications, weather limits, navigation and emergency equipment. Conduct transfer only at approved locations and conditions using designated method, trained personnel and required PPE. Mark snap-back zones and control line handling. Maintain life-saving appliances, muster and MOB arrangements. Coordinate permits, SIMOPS, lifting and simultaneous vessel/platform activities. Stop transfer or deck work when limits are exceeded or communication is lost. Report spills and activate response arrangements.

Documents, records and field verification
Vessel assurance, crew certificates, transfer checklist, weather/sea-state records, toolbox talk, permits, drill and pollution response records.
- Verify documents against actual site conditions; retain controlled revision, approval, inspection and closeout evidence.
- Record who checked the control, when, location, result, defects and action closure.

UAE / project application
Apply current UAE federal requirements, relevant emirate authority rules, client/operator standards and approved project procedures. Confirm the latest official edition and applicability. Numeric limits, exposure criteria, design values and inspection intervals must come from current authoritative requirements, manufacturer instructions and competent engineering assessment; this educational reference does not establish universal legal limits.

Practical site scenario
Sea conditions deteriorate during personnel transfer. Suspend transfer, account for personnel, inform bridge/control room and resume only after competent confirmation against approved limits.

Interview and competency questions
1. What are the main hazards and who may be exposed?
2. Which controls are critical, and how will you verify them physically?
3. What documents, permits, competencies or equipment checks are required before work?
4. What conditions require stop-work, escalation or emergency activation?
5. How will you confirm corrective action effectiveness before restart?

