demonstrate that the control was implemented and effective?\n5. Describe a realistic failure scenario, immediate safe response, escalation path and learning review.\n\nModel response approach: explain the sequence (plan - assess - authorize - brief - verify - monitor - close), name the accountable role, cite the approved site procedure/standard rather than guessing a numeric limit, and give a practical example with evidence and corrective-action follow-up.\n\nK. Non-conformance and continual improvement\nClassify findings by actual and potential consequence using the site\'s approved system. Apply immediate risk reduction, assign an accountable owner and due date, identify underlying system causes, verify effectiveness at the workface, and feed lessons into risk assessments, training, procurement, maintenance and management review. Close an action only when objective evidence confirms the risk is controlled.''' ),
  HseTopic(number: 36, title: 'Diving Operations & Underwater Work Safety', content: '''TOPIC 36 – Diving Operations & Underwater Work Safety

Purpose
Prevent fatality and occupational illness during commercial diving and underwater work.

Principal hazards and exposure pathways
Drowning, decompression illness, entanglement, differential pressure, vessel movement, contaminated water, loss of communications and inadequate standby rescue.

Technical requirements and field application
1. Establish task scope, responsible persons, competence requirements and acceptance criteria before work.
2. Complete task-specific risk assessment with workers and affected interfaces; consider routine, non-routine, abnormal and emergency conditions.
3. Apply the hierarchy of controls: eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, then PPE. Verify controls at the workface.
4. Use approved procedures, permits, isolation and competent authorization where applicable. A permit or checklist alone does not make work safe.
5. Confirm equipment suitability, inspection status, environmental conditions, communication and supervision before release.
6. Monitor changing conditions. Stop, make safe and reassess after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, alarms or interface changes.
7. Provide emergency arrangements appropriate to the credible scenario; do not expose untrained personnel to rescue hazards.
8. Record deficiencies with immediate safeguards, accountable owner, due date and effectiveness verification.
9. Resume only after competent review and authorization; communicate revised controls to all affected workers.

Topic-specific controls
Use a competent diving contractor and approved diving plan. Confirm task scope, dive team roles, medical fitness, qualifications, equipment inspection, gas supply, communications, emergency arrangements and weather/current limits. Coordinate vessel and process isolation; control intakes, discharges, thrusters and lifting operations. Maintain standby diver and rescue capability appropriate to plan. Use pre-dive checks, continuous monitoring and clear abort criteria. Treat suspected decompression illness as a medical emergency and activate specialist recompression/medical arrangements. Do not improvise underwater rescue.

Documents, records and field verification
Diving plan, risk assessment, permits, diver competency/medical records, equipment maintenance, gas logs, pre-dive checklist and emergency plan.
- Verify documents against actual site conditions; retain controlled revision, approval, inspection and closeout evidence.
- Record who checked the control, when, location, result, defects and action closure.

UAE / project application
Apply current UAE federal requirements, relevant emirate authority rules, client/operator standards and approved project procedures. Confirm the latest official edition and applicability. Numeric limits, exposure criteria, design values and inspection intervals must come from current authoritative requirements, manufacturer instructions and competent engineering assessment; this educational reference does not establish universal legal limits.

Practical site scenario
Communications fail during a dive. Surface/abort according to dive plan, deploy standby response, secure conflicting operations and investigate before further diving.

Interview and competency questions
1. What are the main hazards and who may be exposed?
2. Which controls are critical, and how will you verify them physically?
3. What documents, permits, competencies or equipment checks are required before work?
4. What conditions require stop-work, escalation or emergency activation?
5. How will you confirm corrective action effectiveness before restart?

Field assessment
Ask the learner to prepare a task risk assessment, identify critical barriers, conduct a pre-start field verification, explain emergency actions and demonstrate a safe handover/closeout.\n\nFIELD-READY GOLD STANDARD EXPANSION\nDiving: require competent dive contractor, approved dive plan, medical fitness, surface support, communications, standby diver, gas supply and emergency/recompression arrangements appropriate to operation. Assess currents, visibility, entanglement, vessel/propeller interface and contaminated water. No ad hoc diving or unplanned rescue.''\n\nDETAILED GOLD STANDARD FIELD IMPLEMENTATION - TOPIC 36\n\nA. Planning and applicability\nDefine the work boundary, lifecycle stage, people exposed, simultaneous activities, operating status and interfaces before mobilization. Identify applicable UAE federal requirements, emirate authority rules, client/operator standards, contract specifications and approved engineering documents. Maintain a compliance register with source, applicability rationale, owner, evidence and review date. Do not treat guidance or a client rule as legislation; verify current editions and site-specific requirements.\n\nB. Competence and accountability\nThe responsible manager provides resources and confirms the approved method. The supervisor briefs the work party, checks competency and verifies controls at the workface. The HSE Officer advises, samples compliance, records deficiencies and escalates imminent danger; operational control remains with the designated line authority. Workers stop and report unsafe conditions. Appoint competent specialists where engineering, inspection, medical, environmental or emergency expertise is required.\n\nC. Task risk assessment\nBreak the job into sequential steps. For each step identify routine, non-routine and foreseeable abnormal conditions; exposed persons; initiating events; credible consequences; existing barriers; residual risk; and named action owners. Apply elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative controls and PPE in that order. Record assumptions, interfaces and trigger conditions requiring reassessment. Revalidate after scope, personnel, equipment, weather, site condition or simultaneous-operations changes.\n\nD. Authorization and work pack\nBefore work, confirm approved risk assessment/JSA, method statement, permits where required, drawings, isolation certificates, competency records, equipment certificates, inspection status, emergency arrangements and toolbox-talk attendance. Check document revision and physical work location. Resolve conflicting permits and SIMOPS through the site coordination process. Authorization is not proof that field conditions remain safe; pause and revalidate when conditions change.\n\nE. Execution and critical-control verification\nConduct a point-of-work walkdown with the crew. Verify each critical control physically, not only by paperwork; identify who checks it, when, and what evidence is retained. Confirm access, exclusion zones, communications, housekeeping, environmental protection and safe access/egress. Use hold points before irreversible or high-consequence steps. If a critical barrier is absent, impaired or uncertain, stop the affected task, make the area safe, notify the responsible supervisor and restart only after documented correction and re-authorization.\n\nF. Inspection, monitoring and records\nSet inspection frequency from law, manufacturer instructions, engineering basis, risk, use intensity and client procedure; never invent a universal interval. Record asset/task identity, location, date, competent inspector, acceptance criteria/source, findings, defects, immediate controls, corrective-action owner and closeout evidence. Quarantine defective equipment where relevant. Retain permits, assessments, briefings, inspection reports, calibration/certification evidence, photographs and closeout records under the approved retention schedule.\n\nG. Emergency and abnormal conditions\nIdentify c